# The ONUM Variable Axis

Written by Jake Maddox
=> releases.tsv	Version 1.00

## Introduction

The ONUM variable axis is used for interpolation between lining and old-style numbers, allowing users to choose their desired figure height. This level of control was previously only available to the type designer.

This variable axis is intended to replace the onum and lnum OpenType features.

## Conventions

* ONUM=0, the minimum value, represents lining figures.
* ONUM=1, the maximum value, represents old-style figures, with non-ascending numerals typically at the x-height of the typeface.

## CSS

Traditionally, this CSS code was used to choose between lining and old-style figures:

```code
font-variant-numeric: lining-nums;
font-variant-numeric: oldstyle-nums;
```

Using the ONUM axis, here is the new code:

```code
font-variation-settings: "ONUM" 0; /* lining figures */
font-variation-settings: "ONUM" 1; /* x-height old-style figures */
```

The advantage of the ONUM axis is that it is also possible to go between 0 and 1. Here is an example:

```code
font-variation-settings: "ONUM" 0.5;
```
